lug":"scott-ryan","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Slade Brockman","party":"DPRES","person_id":"tvfy_10900","person_slug":"slade-brockman","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Stirling Griff","party":"Centre Alliance","person_id":"tvfy_10851","person_slug":"stirling-griff","vote":"no"},{"name":"Sue Lines","party":"PRES","person_id":"tvfy_10782","person_slug":"sue-lines","vote":"no"},{"name":"Tim Ayres","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10946","person_slug":"tim-ayres","vote":"no"},{"name":"Zed Seselja","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10822","person_slug":"zed-seselja","vote":"aye"}],"name":"Motions - Scientific Research - Reflect","noes":27,"question":"The majority voted in favour of a [motion](https://www.openaustralia.org.au/senate/?id=2020-12-03.75.2) introduced by Queensland Senator [Gerard Rennick](https://theyvoteforyou.org.au/people/senate/queensland/gerard_rennick) (Liberal), which means it passed. Motions like these don't make any legal changes by themselves, but are politically influential because they represent the will of the Senate. ### Motion text > *That the Senate—* > > *(a) recognises that:* > >> *(i) there are many approaches to epistemology, including rationalism, empiricism and scepticism,* >> >> *(ii) data backed up by robust quality assurance is the recorded evidence that scientific theory often relies on,* >> >> *(iii) all hypotheses and theories should be tested against observations that are measured and recorded consistently and accurately,* >> >> *(iv) data that does not demonstrate cause and effect is open to scrutiny, and* >> >> *(v) as attributed to the 1965 Nobel Prize in Physics winner, Richard Feynman, 'I would rather have questions that can't be answered than answers that can't be questioned'; and* > > *(b) calls on the Senate to reflect on these principles when reviewing scientific research and considering policy.*","result":"affirmative","slug":"division-federal-senate-6779","source_url":"https://theyvoteforyou.org.au/divisions/senate/2020-12-03/9"}
